==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{chiefly|British}} a [[rag]] worn around the foot in place of a [[sock]] by a [[tramp]] or [[vagrant]]
# the tramp himself, ''subsequently generalized to: ''
# a [[worthless]] [[nobody]] from the [[dreg]]s of [[society]]
#* '''2007''', J. K. Rowling, ''Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows'', USA: Arthur A. Levine Books (2007), page 674,
#*: ''"I know James Potter's an arrogant '''toerag'''," she said, cutting across Snape.''

# 'Toerag' is a rude and vulger term used as an insult by the English.It is more acceptable, for those who do not know its true meaning, than the common Anglo-Saxon words for female genitalia used in the same context.It was a common insult used in the slums of London since at least the early 19th.Century.
Derivation: 'toerag' is the sanitised spelling of tow-rag. Tow is a coarse hemp or flax fibre that was used to stuff the body orifices after death to prevent the leakage of fluids prior to burial.



